::U5L2Q01::A communicable disease is {=an illness capable of spreading through a transmission route ~every illness caused by heredity ~a confirmed diagnosis without evidence ~a treatment method}

::U5L2Q02::A pathogen is {=an organism or agent capable of causing disease ~a prevention strategy ~a symptom only ~a public-health agency}

::U5L2Q03::A virus reproduces {=inside host cells ~only on clean surfaces ~without a host ~only in food}

::U5L2Q04::Which statement about bacteria is accurate? {=Some bacteria can cause disease ~All bacteria are viruses ~Every bacterium causes illness ~Antibiotics treat every pathogen}

::U5L2Q05::A parasite is an organism that {=lives on or in a host and obtains resources from it ~always spreads through droplets ~is a type of vaccine ~cannot cause infection}

::U5L2Q06::Transmission means {=movement of a pathogen between sources and susceptible hosts ~professional identification of a condition ~the time before symptoms ~the body's resistance}

::U5L2Q07::Direct-contact transmission involves {=person-to-person physical contact ~a contaminated object only ~a mosquito vector only ~suspended particles only}

::U5L2Q08::Indirect contact may occur through {=a contaminated object or surface ~heredity ~vaccination ~diagnosis}

::U5L2Q09::Droplet transmission involves {=short-range respiratory particles ~only contaminated food ~a genetic trait ~a medical treatment}

::U5L2Q10::Airborne transmission involves particles that {=can remain suspended in air ~must be carried by an insect ~exist only on skin ~cannot be reduced by ventilation}

::U5L2Q11::A vector is {=an organism that carries a pathogen between hosts ~a laboratory diagnosis ~a type of symptom ~a treatment plan}

::U5L2Q12::Exposure means {=contact with a possible source without proving infection ~confirmed infection in every case ~a qualified diagnosis ~successful treatment}

::U5L2Q13::Infection is {=invasion and multiplication of a pathogen ~any possible exposure ~a prevention method ~a community policy}

::U5L2Q14::The incubation period is {=the time between infection and symptom onset ~the length of treatment only ~the time required for vaccination ~the period after recovery}

::U5L2Q15::Asymptomatic means {=infected without noticeable symptoms ~exposed but never infected ~unable to transmit infection ~fully diagnosed without testing}

::U5L2Q16::Which conclusion is supported when a person attended an event where illness may have spread? {=The person may have been exposed ~The person definitely has the disease ~The person needs antibiotics ~Every attendee is contagious}

::U5L2Q17::Symptoms alone generally do not prove {=a specific diagnosis ~that the person feels unwell ~that evaluation may be useful ~that monitoring may be appropriate}

::U5L2Q18::Testing is used to {=detect evidence related to a health condition ~guarantee prevention ~replace all professional evaluation ~identify every possible disease instantly}

::U5L2Q19::Diagnosis is {=qualified identification of a health condition ~any student's guess from symptoms ~the same as exposure ~a community prevention strategy}

::U5L2Q20::Vaccination can reduce disease risk by {=developing protection against particular infections ~treating every active infection ~replacing hygiene ~proving a diagnosis}

::U5L2Q21::Hand hygiene most directly helps interrupt {=contact transmission ~heredity ~diagnosis ~incubation}

::U5L2Q22::Ventilation can help reduce some {=respiratory transmission ~genetic conditions ~vector bites ~food-storage errors}

::U5L2Q23::Safe food storage is intended to reduce {=foodborne disease risk ~airborne transmission only ~hereditary risk ~the need for testing}

::U5L2Q24::Protective clothing and vector control can reduce {=some vector-borne disease risk ~all bacterial resistance ~every respiratory illness ~the incubation period}

::U5L2Q25::Antibiotics are appropriate for {=certain bacterial infections when professionally directed ~all viral infections ~every possible exposure ~any symptom without evaluation}

::U5L2Q26::Why should antibiotics be used only as directed? {=Incorrect use can be ineffective and contribute to harm or resistance ~They prevent every virus ~They replace testing ~They guarantee immunity}

::U5L2Q27::Sharing drink containers creates {=a possible indirect-contact or saliva exposure route ~proof that everyone is infected ~an airborne route only ~a diagnosis}

::U5L2Q28::Which combines personal and community prevention? {=Hand hygiene plus appropriate cleaning and public-health guidance ~Ignoring symptoms plus sharing objects ~Self-diagnosis plus unnecessary antibiotics ~Waiting for spread before acting}

::U5L2Q29::Which evidence-to-conclusion statement is strongest? {=Because food was stored improperly and several people became ill, foodborne exposure is possible, but testing is needed for diagnosis ~Everyone definitely has the same disease ~The food cannot be involved ~Antibiotics are required for all attendees}

::U5L2Q30::Why is stigma inappropriate during a communicable-disease event? {=Exposure or infection is a health issue, and respectful evidence-based action supports prevention and care ~Blame improves testing accuracy ~Stigma proves who caused spread ~Respect prevents public-health action}
